# Git commit and push standards

## When to commit or push

- **Commit** only when the user explicitly asks, or when a user rule requires it.
- **Push** only when the user explicitly asks. Never force-push to `main`/`master` without explicit request and a warning.
- Never commit secrets (`.env`, keys, tokens, credentials). Warn if the user tries to include them.

## Before every commit

Run in parallel (read-only inspection):

1. `git status` — untracked and modified files
2. `git diff` and `git diff --staged` — what will be committed
3. `git log -10 --oneline` — match existing message style

Stage only files that belong to this change. Do not bundle unrelated work.

## Commit message format (Conventional Commits)

```
<type>(<optional scope>): <imperative summary ≤ 72 chars>

<blank line>

<body: what changed and why, not a file list>

<optional footer: Breaking change:, Fixes #123>
```

### Types

| Type | Use when |
|------|----------|
| `feat` | New user-facing capability |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `style` | Formatting, no logic change |
| `refactor` | Code change, same behavior |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |
| `test` | Tests only |
| `build` | Build, deps, CI |
| `chore` | Tooling, config, housekeeping |

### Writing rules

- **Subject:** imperative mood ("add" not "added"), no trailing period, ≤ 72 characters.
- **Body:** complete sentences; explain **why** and **impact**, not every filename.
- **Scope:** optional, short (`i18n`, `contact`, `deploy`, `analytics`).
- One logical change per commit unless the user asks to squash.

### Examples

```
# Weak
fix stuff
update files
WIP

# Strong
fix(contact): prevent empty subject in mailto prefilled body

The contact form allowed submit with whitespace-only subject.
Trim and validate before building the mailto URL.

feat(analytics): wire Vercel Analytics and Speed Insights in root layout

Enables production traffic and Web Vitals without custom instrumentation.
Dashboard toggles still required in Vercel project settings.
```

## Commit command

- Use a HEREDOC for the message so newlines are preserved.
- Never use `-i` flags (`git add -i`, `git rebase -i`).
- Never skip hooks (`--no-verify`) unless the user explicitly requests it.
- Never `git commit --amend` unless all safety conditions in the user commit rule are met.

## Before every push

1. Confirm branch: `git status` (ahead/behind remote).
2. Summarize commits to push: `git log origin/<base>..HEAD --oneline`.
3. For PRs, use `gh pr create` with Summary + Test plan sections.

## Push rules

- Use `git push -u origin HEAD` when the branch has no upstream.
- Do not force-push unless the user explicitly requests it; warn on `main`/`master`.
- After push, report branch name and commit range pushed.

## PR description template

```markdown
## Summary
- <1–3 bullets: outcome for reviewer>

## Test plan
- [ ] `npm run lint`
- [ ] `npm run build`
- [ ] <manual checks relevant to this change>
```
